# Contributing to Cartord

Thanks for your interest in improving Cartord. We welcome contributions from engineers, operators, testers, and anyone with ideas to make the project more useful, reliable, and production-ready.

## Ways to contribute

- Report bugs and reproducible issues
- Suggest improvements and feature ideas
- Improve documentation and examples
- Submit code, tests, or configuration updates
- Help with troubleshooting and operational feedback

## Before you start

1. Review the project documentation in the repository README.
2. Make sure your issue or change is aligned with the current architecture and roadmap.
3. For large changes, open or reference an issue first so the maintainers can review the design before implementation.

## Local setup

This repository is organized as a set of Python microservices with shared infrastructure definitions. Typical local development uses Docker Compose and Makefile-based commands.

1. Clone the repository.
2. Create and activate a Python environment if you are working on service code.
3. Install service dependencies as needed for the work you are touching.
4. Use Docker Compose or the provided Makefile targets to run the stack locally.
5. Run targeted tests for the service or code path you changed.

See the root README for the supported workflow and environment references.

## Development workflow

- Create a branch for your work.
- Keep changes focused on a single issue or feature.
- Prefer small, readable pull requests with clear descriptions.
- Add or update tests when behavior changes.
- Update documentation when user-facing or operational workflows change.

## Coding standards

- Follow the conventions already used within the relevant service.
- Keep new code readable, explicit, and easy to reason about.
- Use type hints and validation patterns consistent with the existing Python code.
- Avoid introducing unnecessary dependencies or broad refactors unless they are required for the fix.
- Preserve service boundaries and the event-driven design discussed in the project documentation.

## Testing

Before opening a pull request, validate the area you changed.

Recommended checks:

- Unit tests for the specific service or module
- Linting or formatting if the project or service includes it
- Local integration checks for any changed API or event flow

When possible, include the exact commands used and the outcome in your PR description.

## Pull request guidelines

- Use a clear title that describes the change.
- Include a summary of the problem being solved.
- Explain any user-visible changes, migration steps, or operational impact.
- Link the related issue if one exists.
- Include screenshots or logs when they help explain a fix.

## Community expectations

Please be respectful, constructive, and collaborative. We expect contributors to uphold the project Code of Conduct and to keep discussions focused on solving problems and improving the system.

## Questions

If you have questions about an issue, design direction, or contribution approach, please open a discussion or issue in the repository before making a large change.
